---
import floors from '../../data/probe/floor.json'
import probe from '../../data/probe/results2.json'
import { pearson, spearman } from '../lib/metrics.js'

// Beat 2 states correlations, so like the beats either side of it every figure is
// recomputed here from the cited rows rather than carried across from a spec. The rate of
// the fall is results2.json's pow_a, the fitted exponent beat 3 has just measured; the
// floor is floor.json's. The one multiple printed is floor.json's, to the one place both
// files agree on for that subject.
const byArticle = new Map(probe.map((r) => [r.article, r]))
const rows = floors.map((r) => {
  const p = byArticle.get(r.article)
  if (!p) throw new Error(`no results2.json row for ${r.article}`)
  return { article: r.article, peak: r.peak, floor: r.floor, exponent: p.pow_a }
})

const logPeakAll = probe.map((r) => Math.log10(r.peak))
const fmt = (v) => `${v < 0 ? '−' : '+'}${Math.abs(v).toFixed(2)}`

// The measurement the beat is taking back: days to half the lift, against peak size, over
// every event the probe measured.
const rHalf = fmt(pearson(logPeakAll, probe.map((r) => r.t50)))
const big = probe.reduce((a, b) => (b.peak > a.peak ? b : a))
const small = probe.reduce((a, b) => (b.peak < a.peak ? b : a))

// The same question against the fitted exponent, which is the rate of the whole fall.
const rFall = fmt(pearson(logPeakAll, probe.map((r) => r.pow_a)))
const pFall = fmt(spearman(logPeakAll, probe.map((r) => r.pow_a)))

// The exponent against the floor, on the subjects that carry one.
const logPeak = rows.map((r) => Math.log10(r.peak))
const exponents = rows.map((r) => r.exponent)
const floorOf = rows.map((r) => r.floor)
const rExpFloor = fmt(pearson(exponents, floorOf))
const pExpFloor = fmt(spearman(exponents, floorOf))
const rPeakFloor = fmt(pearson(logPeak, floorOf))
const pPeakFloor = fmt(spearman(logPeak, floorOf))

// The tail a straight line spends its fit on, as a multiple of that subject's own clean
// baseline. Printed to one place, which is a place floor.json and dataset.json agree on
// for this row.
const highest = (1 + Math.max(...floorOf)).toFixed(1)

const ladder = JSON.stringify(rows.map((r) => ({
  article: r.article,
  peak: r.peak,
  floor: r.floor,
  exponent: r.exponent,
})))
---

<section class="beat">
  <p class="body">
    The obvious answer is the fall. A spike that large should take longer to come down.
  </p>

  <p class="body">
    This page used to answer that with days to half the lift, and days to half says no. The
    largest peak in the set is half gone in {big.t50} days and the smallest in {small.t50},
    and across all {probe.length} events the two run together at r = {rHalf}. That is one
    crossing of one threshold. It is not the rate of the fall.
  </p>

  <p class="body">
    The rate is the exponent fitted above, and against the exponent the spike does sort the
    fall. The same {probe.length} events give r = {rFall} on the values and ρ = {pFall} on
    the ranks. The bigger the spike, the steeper the curve under it.
  </p>

  <figure class="fig">
    <div class="heads">
      <span class="head start">The fall</span>
      <span class="head mid">The floor</span>
      <span class="head end">The spike</span>
    </div>
    <div class="ends top">
      <span class="start">steepest exponent</span>
      <span class="mid">furthest below its baseline</span>
      <span class="end">largest peak</span>
    </div>
    <canvas
      id="ladder"
      class="ladder"
      data-rows={ladder}
      role="img"
      aria-label="Every subject that carries a clean baseline, threaded between three axes: its position when the set is ordered by the exponent of its fall, by its floor, and by the size of its peak."
    ></canvas>
    <div class="ends bottom">
      <span class="start">flattest exponent</span>
      <span class="mid">furthest above it</span>
      <span class="end">smallest peak</span>
    </div>
    <figcaption>
      The {rows.length} subjects that carry a clean baseline, threaded between three orders
      of the same set. A thread's height on an axis is its place in that order, not its
      value. The colour is the middle axis, so it grades down the middle by construction.
      Read the left half against the right: the same subjects, the same floor order, sorted
      once by the fall and once by the spike.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p class="body">
    The exponent sorts the floor as well. A straight line reads r = {rExpFloor} across the {rows.length} subjects that carry one, which is small enough to pass over. On the ranks the
    same subjects give ρ = {pExpFloor}. The floor is heavy tailed, the highest of them ends
    the year at {highest} times its own baseline, and a straight line spends its fit out
    there.
  </p>

  <p class="body">
    So the fall is not removed here. What holds is the other suspect. Spike size does not
    sort the floor on either measure, r = {rPeakFloor} and ρ = {pPeakFloor}, and that is the
    reading the rest of this page rests on.
  </p>
</section>

<script>
  import { mountLadder } from '../scripts/ladder.js'

  const canvas = document.querySelector('#ladder')
  if (canvas) mountLadder(canvas, JSON.parse(canvas.dataset.rows))
</script>

<style>
